  "methodology": "FPI's portfolio is mapped from the FY2025 10-K Schedule III \"Combined Real Estate and Accumulated Depreciation\" table, which lists 121 line items aggregating the company's owned farmland after the 2025 disposition program (60 properties / $90.2M sold). Total gross cost basis $641,861K and net book $615,078K are reconciled against the schedule's totals row; small (~1%) parsing residuals are due to a few rows where the regex didn't perfectly capture all 10 numeric tokens. Per-property acres are not disclosed in Schedule III; per-state acre estimates are allocated based on FPI's historic disclosures and rough state-level totals (Illinois ~12K, California ~4.5K, Nebraska ~5K, South Carolina ~5.5K, Colorado ~8K, Louisiana ~4K, Texas ~8.5K, Missouri/Indiana ~3K, Other 60-farm aggregate ~21K \u2014 totaling ~71.6K acres post-2025 disposals). Per-line-item acres are pro-rata allocated within each state by net book value.\n\nFMV is built bottoms-up by mapping these state-level acres to nine crop / region buckets using USDA NASS 2024 state averages plus Iowa State CARD survey premium for Class-A Illinois ground. The largest single value driver is the Illinois Class-A allocation (12K acres \u00d7 $11K/acre = $132M). Implied NAV/share at FMV deducts net debt (debt + Series A pref RNCI \u2212 cash) and divides by current diluted shares. FPI's premium-to-book is much smaller than LAND's because Midwest row-crop tracks closer to USDA-published values than CA permanent crops do."
}